  "reasoning": "The patient's primary symptoms are constipation, worsening belly pain, and significant fatigue/weakness. Reviewing the reference cases, Reference [1] explicitly matches this presentation ('belly pain and constipation' plus 'feeling very weak') and provides the diagnosis of typhoid. Reference [6] further supports this by linking stomachache, constipation/diarrhea, and persistent tiredness to typhoid. Alternative diagnoses do not align as closely: peptic ulcer disease and gastroesophageal reflux disease typically feature burning epigastric pain, acid reflux, or chest discomfort; urinary tract infections present with urinary urgency and dysuria; diabetes involves polydipsia and polyuria; and conditions like malaria, dengue, or pneumonia focus on fever, respiratory symptoms, or severe joint/head pain. Given the specific combination of abdominal pain, altered bowel habits (constipation), and systemic weakness, typhoid is the most accurate diagnosis supported by the clinical facts and reference corpus.",
